ZAICODE

ROLE

You are the implementation and integration agent.

Build the first working ZAICODE foundation on top of the current official upstream ZCode source repository:

https://github.com/zai-org/ZCode

This is a multi-milestone autonomous implementation handoff.

Do not stop after repository installation or after producing an architectural report. Continue through the milestones sequentially while eligible work remains.

Do not ask for "continue?" between normal milestones.

Stop only for a genuine hard blocker:

* required operator credential or interactive authentication that cannot be completed safely;
* destructive migration requiring explicit operator approval;
* upstream integrity or licensing problem;
* missing dependency that cannot reasonably be installed;
* architecture discovery proving that the requested integration cannot be implemented without replacing a major upstream subsystem.

PRIMARY GOAL

Use ZCode as the technical foundation for ZAICODE.

ZAICODE should evolve into a local coding-agent workspace where multiple named agents can be configured, observed, assigned work, queued, resumed, and eventually orchestrated from one UI.

The first implementation must establish:

1. isolated local ZAICODE source installation;
2. verified upstream development environment;
3. architecture map of the existing ZCode agent/subagent/task/queue/runtime systems;
4. ZAICODE-specific UI product contract in root `UI.md`;
5. first ZAICODE agent-management layer using existing ZCode primitives wherever possible;
6. first useful Agent Queue UI;
7. persistent agent definitions and observable execution state;
8. clean verification and continuation state for future work.

UPSTREAM BASELINE

Treat the currently cloned official `zai-org/ZCode` main branch as the source of truth unless repository metadata clearly indicates another supported development branch.

At the beginning, record:

* upstream repository URL;
* current branch;
* exact commit SHA;
* package version;
* Node version required by `mise.toml`;
* pnpm version required by `mise.toml`;
* license and NOTICE obligations.

Do not silently vendor an unknown third-party fork.

INSTALLATION SAFETY

The user already uses ZCode.

Do not overwrite, uninstall, corrupt, reset, migrate, or repurpose the user's existing production ZCode installation or account configuration.

Do not destructively modify existing ZCode runtime state under the user's profile.

Create a separate development checkout/workspace for ZAICODE.

The development source checkout and the installed production ZCode application must be treated as separate systems.

Do not reuse production databases for development tests when an isolated test database or profile can be used.

Do not expose secrets, OAuth tokens, API keys, account identifiers, or credentials in repository files, logs, fixtures, screenshots, or documentation.

MILESTONE 1 — BOOTSTRAP ZAICODE

Clone the official ZCode repository into a dedicated ZAICODE development directory.

Before changing source:

1. inspect:

   * `README.en.md`
   * `AGENTS.md`
   * `DESIGN.md`
   * `CONTEXT.md`
   * `NOTICE.md`
   * `LICENSE`
   * `mise.toml`
   * root `package.json`
   * workspace configuration;

2. determine the exact supported toolchain;

3. install the required pnpm version without unnecessarily replacing unrelated global tooling;

4. run the upstream-supported bootstrap path;

5. validate at least one local development interface:

   * Desktop preferred on Windows;
   * Web is acceptable as an additional verification;
   * Agent CLI may be validated separately.

Do not change application branding before proving an untouched upstream development build can start.

Create a baseline receipt containing:

* commit SHA;
* dependency installation result;
* bootstrap result;
* startup result;
* existing test/typecheck/lint failures, if any.

Do not classify pre-existing upstream failures as ZAICODE regressions.

MILESTONE 2 — ARCHITECTURE DISCOVERY

Before implementing ZAICODE orchestration, trace the actual source ownership of:

* agent definitions;
* built-in agents;
* custom agents;
* subagents;
* subagent model overrides;
* session lifecycle;
* background tasks;
* task execution;
* pending tasks;
* queue semantics;
* queue persistence;
* cancellation;
* retry behavior;
* completion notifications;
* workspace identity;
* agent permissions;
* model/provider selection;
* runtime-to-UI event propagation;
* RPC boundaries;
* persistence/database ownership.

Search the repository rather than inferring behavior from UI labels.

Produce an internal architecture note such as:

`docs/ZAICODE_ARCHITECTURE.md`

It must identify concrete source files/classes/services/stores/interfaces responsible for the above.

For every queue-like structure discovered, classify it as one of:

* execution queue;
* UI pending-input queue;
* background-task queue;
* notification queue;
* session queue;
* transient in-memory ordering mechanism;
* persistent work queue;
* unrelated queue.

Do not call something the "ZAICODE task queue" merely because a symbol contains the word `queue`.

Determine whether the existing ZCode architecture already provides enough primitives for a durable operator-facing job queue.

PREFER REUSE OVER DUPLICATION.

If an existing queue/runtime can own ZAICODE jobs safely, extend that path.

If no suitable durable queue exists, add the smallest explicit orchestration layer possible with one authoritative state owner.

Do not introduce a second competing session state machine.

MILESTONE 3 — ZAICODE PRODUCT IDENTITY

Introduce ZAICODE as a derived product layer while preserving upstream attribution and licensing requirements.

Avoid a massive rename wave.

Prefer centralized branding/product metadata so future upstream rebases remain feasible.

At minimum define a single source of truth for:

* product display name: ZAICODE;
* upstream identity/provenance: ZCode by Z.ai;
* ZAICODE-specific feature flags or product capabilities where needed.

Do not mechanically rename internal package names, protocol identifiers, persistence schemas, environment variables, or RPC names merely for cosmetic branding.

Internal names should remain upstream-compatible unless changing them has a functional reason.

UPSTREAM UPDATEABILITY IS AN INVARIANT.

MILESTONE 4 — CREATE ROOT UI.md

Create:

`UI.md`

This file is the ZAICODE product-interface contract.

It supplements upstream `DESIGN.md`; it does not replace or duplicate the design system.

`DESIGN.md` remains authoritative for component styling, typography tokens, colors, themes, spacing, responsive behavior, accessibility conventions, and supported platform behavior.

`UI.md` must define ZAICODE-specific information architecture and operator workflows.

At minimum include:

# ZAICODE UI

## Product Purpose

ZAICODE is an operator-oriented multi-agent coding workspace.

The UI should make agent state, queued work, active execution, blockers, results, and ownership obvious without forcing the operator to open multiple dialogs.

## Core Principles

* one primary workspace;
* dense but readable;
* desktop-first;
* minimal modal interaction;
* keyboard-friendly;
* no unnecessary combobox-heavy configuration;
* critical execution state always visible;
* explicit agent ownership;
* explicit queue state;
* explicit blocked state;
* explicit provider/model identity;
* no hidden destructive actions;
* persistent operator context across restart.

## Main Workspace

Define the intended main-screen regions:

### Navigation / Workspace

Project/workspace selection and high-level ZAICODE navigation.

### Agent Roster

Visible list of configured agents.

Each row/card should expose:

* name;
* role;
* status;
* model;
* provider;
* current job;
* queue depth where applicable;
* last activity;
* error/blocker indicator.

### Work Queue

Operator-facing ordered task queue.

Required task states:

* Draft
* Queued
* Ready
* Running
* Waiting
* Blocked
* Completed
* Failed
* Cancelled

Do not add states that the runtime cannot actually distinguish.

### Active Work

Show currently executing agent jobs and meaningful progress/runtime events.

### Inspector

Selected agent/job details without forcing navigation away from the queue.

### Event / Result Surface

Readable execution results, important tool events, errors, and completion summaries.

## Agent Management

Specify:

* Create Agent;
* Rename Agent;
* Edit Role;
* Edit Instructions;
* Choose Provider;
* Choose Model;
* Enable/Disable;
* Duplicate;
* Delete;
* Run;
* Pause only if runtime supports real pause semantics;
* Cancel;
* Queue Task.

Do not implement fake Pause if the engine only supports Cancel.

## Queue Interaction

Specify:

* add task;
* edit pending task;
* reorder eligible tasks;
* dispatch;
* cancel;
* retry failed;
* resume blocked task when possible;
* inspect history;
* filter by agent/status/workspace;
* preserve queue across application restart when persistence is supported.

## Agent Status Vocabulary

Use precise runtime-backed states.

Suggested display vocabulary:

* Idle
* Queued
* Starting
* Running
* Waiting
* Blocked
* Completed
* Failed
* Offline

Map every displayed status to an authoritative runtime state.

## Model and Provider Visibility

The UI must make it possible to see which model/provider an agent will actually use.

Do not show a configured model as active when runtime fallback selected something else.

## Permissions

Dangerous or privileged tool actions must respect the existing permission system.

Subagents must not silently gain broader permissions than their parent or configured profile.

## Persistence

Document which ZAICODE UI state is:

* runtime-authoritative;
* persisted configuration;
* local UI preference;
* derived display state.

## Keyboard Workflow

Define useful shortcuts only after checking for collisions with existing ZCode shortcuts.

## Responsive Behavior

Preserve usable Web behavior but prioritize the Windows desktop workspace.

## Non-Goals

For the first ZAICODE milestone:

* no distributed multi-machine scheduler;
* no arbitrary agent nesting;
* no visual node editor;
* no complex DAG workflow engine;
* no autonomous infinite execution;
* no replacement of ZCode's model/provider stack;
* no rewrite of the terminal;
* no replacement of upstream permission architecture.

MILESTONE 5 — AGENT DEFINITION LAYER

Implement the smallest clean ZAICODE agent-definition layer compatible with the existing ZCode agent/subagent architecture.

An agent definition should conceptually support:

* stable ID;
* display name;
* role;
* instructions;
* enabled state;
* provider reference;
* model reference;
* optional reasoning/thinking configuration only when supported by that provider/model;
* capability/tool policy;
* creation/update timestamps where useful.

Avoid copying provider configuration or credentials into ZAICODE agent definitions.

Reference existing provider/model identities.

Use schema validation.

Persist definitions through an existing suitable ZCode persistence/service path when possible.

Do not create direct filesystem writes from React UI components.

Respect existing package/service/RPC boundaries.

MILESTONE 6 — FIRST DEFAULT ZAICODE AGENTS

Create a small initial set of useful built-in ZAICODE agent templates.

Keep the system extensible; do not hardwire application logic around these names.

Initial templates:

1. Coordinator
   Purpose:

   * receives operator work;
   * decomposes tasks when appropriate;
   * assigns work to eligible agents;
   * summarizes state and results.

2. Implementer
   Purpose:

   * implementation and repair work;
   * may modify code within granted permissions.

3. Auditor
   Purpose:

   * read-oriented inspection;
   * verification;
   * architecture and regression review.

4. Researcher
   Purpose:

   * repository exploration;
   * dependency/API/documentation investigation;
   * should default to conservative write permissions.

These are templates, not magical privileged identities.

The user must be able to create additional agents later.

MILESTONE 7 — ZAICODE WORK QUEUE

Implement the first operator-facing ZAICODE queue.

First inspect whether ZCode already has an authoritative execution/background-task queue suitable for extension.

If yes:

* expose and extend it instead of duplicating state.

If no:

* create a minimal durable queue service at the appropriate service layer.

Minimum job model:

* id;
* workspace identity;
* agent ID;
* title;
* task/instructions;
* status;
* priority or explicit ordering;
* created time;
* started time;
* finished time;
* result summary;
* error/blocker reason;
* associated session/run identity;
* retry lineage where applicable.

Required invariants:

* a job has one authoritative owner;
* the same queued job cannot be dispatched twice accidentally;
* restart does not silently convert Running into Completed;
* stale runs cannot overwrite a newer retry;
* cancellation is idempotent;
* completion is idempotent;
* retries create traceable lineage;
* workspace identity is preserved;
* task ordering is deterministic;
* UI state is not treated as execution truth;
* completion notification failure must not silently lose terminal job state.

Prefer explicit lifecycle transitions over booleans.

MILESTONE 8 — AGENTS + QUEUE UI

Add the first ZAICODE UI surface using existing ZCode shared UI architecture and design-system components.

Do not create an unrelated standalone frontend.

Target a single primary ZAICODE workspace rather than many configuration windows.

The first usable UI should include:

LEFT OR COMPACT SIDE REGION

* Agent roster.

CENTER

* Queue and active work.

RIGHT OR COLLAPSIBLE INSPECTOR

* selected agent or selected job.

Provide:

* agent status;
* model/provider identity;
* queued jobs;
* running job;
* result/error summary;
* task creation;
* assignment;
* basic queue reordering where safe;
* cancel/retry where supported;
* agent create/edit flow.

Prefer tabs or integrated panels over chains of modal dialogs.

Avoid unnecessary dropdowns when radio groups, segmented controls, searchable lists, inline controls, or direct selections are clearer.

Do not violate upstream `DESIGN.md`.

MILESTONE 9 — ORCHESTRATION PROBE

After the basic queue and agent layer work, implement only a bounded orchestration proof.

Goal:

The Coordinator can take one operator task and dispatch at least one child job to another configured ZAICODE agent through the real queue/runtime path.

It must be observable in the UI.

The proof must demonstrate:

operator task
→ coordinator ownership
→ child job creation
→ queue admission
→ target agent execution
→ terminal state
→ result returned or linked to coordinator
→ visible completion state

Do not implement recursive agent spawning.

Do not implement unlimited parallelism.

Use a conservative configurable concurrency limit.

Concurrency must never be inferred only from UI state.

MILESTONE 10 — FAILURE AND RECOVERY

Test at minimum:

* application restart with queued jobs;
* restart while a job was Running;
* agent disabled while jobs are queued;
* unavailable model/provider;
* agent execution failure;
* cancellation;
* retry;
* duplicate dispatch attempt;
* stale completion arriving after retry;
* workspace switch;
* malformed persisted agent definition;
* malformed persisted queued job;
* background completion notification arriving late;
* missing result payload;
* provider/model fallback if upstream supports fallback.

Terminal state must remain explainable.

Never silently turn an unknown outcome into success.

MILESTONE 11 — TESTING

Follow upstream repository requirements.

At minimum execute the applicable:

* focused unit tests;
* service tests;
* UI tests;
* E2E tests for new interaction behavior;
* typecheck;
* lint;
* architecture checks required by the repository;
* build of changed packages;
* local Desktop smoke test.

Record commands and actual outcomes.

Do not claim tests passed if they were skipped.

MILESTONE 12 — DOCUMENTATION

Update or add concise ZAICODE documentation covering:

* how to bootstrap development;
* how to start ZAICODE;
* architecture relationship to upstream ZCode;
* where ZAICODE agent definitions live;
* queue lifecycle;
* how to create an agent;
* how to queue a task;
* current orchestration limitations;
* known upstream limitations;
* upstream update/rebase strategy.

Keep `UI.md` focused on the interface/product contract.

Keep implementation architecture in separate technical documentation.

UPSTREAM COMPATIBILITY RULE

Maintain a clear boundary between:

A. upstream ZCode behavior;
B. ZAICODE extensions;
C. unavoidable upstream modifications.

Prefer additive ZAICODE modules over broad invasive rewrites.

When an upstream file must be modified, keep the delta narrow and document why.

Do not fork core runtime behavior simply because understanding it is inconvenient.

SECURITY / PERMISSIONS

Do not weaken existing permission checks merely to make orchestration easier.

Do not assume subagent permissions are identical to parent-agent permissions.

Verify actual behavior.

Do not expose development Web mode beyond localhost unless explicitly needed.

Do not disable authentication for non-localhost service binding.

Do not place credentials in agent definitions.

Do not automate CAPTCHA or bypass vendor authentication controls.

NON-GOALS

Do not:

* build a replacement LLM provider stack;
* create a new IDE;
* rewrite ZCode from scratch;
* implement distributed agents;
* create recursive autonomous swarms;
* add speculative blockchain/cloud infrastructure;
* add complex workflow DAGs;
* redesign unrelated ZCode screens;
* rename every upstream symbol to ZAICODE;
* spend the milestone on cosmetic branding before functionality works.

DEFINITION OF DONE

This handoff is complete only when all of the following are true:

1. official ZCode source is installed in an isolated ZAICODE development workspace;
2. upstream baseline can be started locally;
3. exact upstream commit and baseline verification are recorded;
4. `UI.md` exists and defines the ZAICODE interface contract;
5. actual ZCode agent/subagent/queue/runtime architecture has been traced;
6. ZAICODE has persistent configurable agent definitions;
7. several initial agent templates exist;
8. the UI can display and manage agents;
9. the UI contains a real operator-facing task queue;
10. at least one queued task can execute through an assigned agent;
11. one bounded Coordinator → worker orchestration flow is demonstrated;
12. queue/job state survives or safely reconciles application restart;
13. duplicate/stale completion protections are verified;
14. typecheck/lint/tests/build results are recorded honestly;
15. current limitations and next highest-value milestone are documented.

FINAL REPORT

Return a compact implementation receipt containing:

STATUS

* DONE / PARTIAL / BLOCKED

UPSTREAM

* repository
* commit
* source version

INSTALLATION

* development path
* toolchain
* bootstrap result
* startup result

ARCHITECTURE

* agent owner
* queue owner
* session owner
* persistence owner
* UI state owner

IMPLEMENTED

* milestones completed
* major files changed

UI

* `UI.md` status
* ZAICODE workspace status
* Agent Roster status
* Queue status
* Inspector status

VALIDATION

* tests
* typecheck
* lint
* build
* Desktop smoke test

KNOWN RISKS

* verified remaining limitations only

UPSTREAM DELTA

* summarize modifications to upstream-owned code separately from additive ZAICODE files

NEXT

* identify the single highest-value continuation target.
